### Playground Link

Not applicable: the report depends on TypeScript checker state, so the repro below is the minimal deterministic form.

### Repro Code

```ts
declare function fill<T, Value>(shape: T, value: Value): { [K in keyof T]: Value };
declare function unfill<U>(mapped: { [K in keyof U]: number }): U;

function cold<T>(shape: T, warmed = false) {
  return fill(shape, 1);
}

function warm<T>(shape: T, warmed = !unfill(fill(shape, 1))) {
  return fill(shape, 1);
}
```

### ESLint Config

```js
export default tseslint.config({
  languageOptions: { parserOptions: { projectService: true } },
  rules: { '@typescript-eslint/no-unnecessary-type-parameters': 'error' },
});
```

### tsconfig

```json
{ "compilerOptions": { "strict": true } }
```

### Expected Result

Neither function is reported. In both, `T` is used by `shape` and by the returned `{ [K in keyof T]: number }`.

### Actual Result

`warm` reports `Type parameter T is used only once in the function signature.` `cold` does not.

### Additional Info

The rule reads `MappedType.templateType`, a checker cache that TypeScript populates lazily. For an instantiated mapped type it stays empty until something relates the type to another generic mapped type; here, checking the `unfill` call does that before the rule visits the return type.

When the cache is populated the rule visits the template instead of the constraint, so uses of a type parameter that appear only in the constraint (`keyof T`) are missed. Whether it is populated depends on what the checker happened to evaluate first, including other rules' queries, so the same signature can be reported or not depending on surrounding code.

Found while working on #12803.

